Description: experimental information-theoretic cryptography and communications suite
 pnp is designed for a world beyond computational security: its schemes'
 security is a probability bound that no amount of computing changes, so it
 holds even if P = NP. At its core it encrypts any file with a one-time pad:
 a key of random bytes as long as the file, read from the machine's hardware
 random number generators (/dev/hwrng and the CPU's RDSEED instruction),
 health-checked as they're read, and combined with XOR.
 .
 pnp FILE writes FILE.pnp and its key FILE.pnp.key; pnp -d FILE.pnp
 decrypts it; pnp alone opens an interactive file browser that shows what
 will be written, and which key on the key drive will be used. Keys can be
 split into shares, and optionally locked with a passphrase (a
 computationally secure extra, not part of the P = NP claim).
 .
 People who meet in person can also seal files (pairs) and sign them
 (groups of 3 or more), with forgery bounded by a small probability rather
 than by hard problems, and send each other one-time-pad messages. Key
 material travels on raw courier drives (pnp-courier-setup, which needs
 gdisk; external spinning disks preferred), and compromised keys are
 reported with kill notices.
 .
 Experimental alpha software, not yet independently audited.
 .
 Members of the pnp group may read /dev/hwrng and use pnp's courier drives.

